VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-001
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe, refractory pain and precipitous functional decline requiring immediate palliative intervention.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, primary caregiver (spouse)
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an acute, severe escalation of nociceptive pain and distressing dyspnea over the last four hours, completely unresponsive to current breakthrough medication. Urgent assessment and intervention were required immediately to provide symptomatic relief and prevent further physical and psychological deterioration prior to the next available in-hours consultation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The pain is everywhere now, burning deep in my bones, and I just can't catch my breath anymore."
History of Present Illness: Over the past three weeks, there has been profound, continuous, and completely unintentional severe weight loss despite adequate nutritional attempts. Anorexia is distinctly coupled with profound early satiety and entirely altered food taste profiles. Over the last 48 hours, refractory, severe nociceptive and deeply burning neuropathic pain has worsened progressively, localized initially to the axial skeleton but now radiating bilaterally. This is accompanied by overwhelming, unremitting systemic fatigue entirely disproportionate to any recent minimal physical activity. In the last 12 hours, the patient developed refractory, severe dyspnea strictly secondary to known malignant pleural effusions. Recurrent, intractable nausea has also emerged today. The progressive, severe functional decline has rapidly rendered the patient strictly bedbound and completely dependent. Significant, progressive neurocognitive decline, explicitly driven by suspected brain metastases or severe metabolic encephalopathy, is noted by the spouse.
Functional Status: Strictly bedbound, totally dependent for all ADLs.
Social/Safety Concerns: Caregiver burnout is highly evident; the spouse is sleep-deprived and overwhelmed by the rapid clinical decline.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 90/60 mmHg | HR: 112 bpm | Temp: 37.4 C | RR: 24 bpm | SpO2: 91% on room air | Wt: 48 Kg
General: Cachectic, frail, and in visible distress due to pain and increased work of breathing.
Physical Exam: Respiratory exam reveals globally diminished breath sounds with dullness to percussion at the bilateral bases. Abdominal and musculoskeletal exams reveal multiple, widespread palpable tumor masses explicitly indicating severe metastatic disease burden. The diagnostic tetrad is fulfilled by observed unintentional severe weight loss, progressive severe pain requiring positional adjustments throughout the exam, and the widespread palpable masses.
Environmental Observations: Hospital bed appropriately placed in the living area; however, trip hazards (loose rugs) are present near the commode. Temperature control is adequate.
Medication Reconciliation: Discrepancies noted in breakthrough analgesia dosing; patient under-dosing due to fear of respiratory depression.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Advanced cancer - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute pulmonary embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to absence of sudden pleuritic chest pain or hemoptysis, and the insidious progressive nature of the dyspnea.

Malignant bowel obstruction - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of active bowel sounds and absence of feculent emesis or severe abdominal distension.

Opioid toxicity - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of tachypnea and tachycardia rather than bradypnea, and pinpoint pupils were not observed.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, unmanageable crisis of severe nociceptive pain and dyspnea secondary to end-stage metastatic disease. The clinical objective evidence—specifically tachycardia, tachypnea, hypoxia, and severe distress despite maximal use of currently prescribed breakthrough analgesia—confirms that the current palliative regimen is failing. Without immediate after-hours intervention to titrate subcutaneous analgesics and anxiolytics, the patient would suffer intolerable, agonizing deterioration and potential terminal agitation overnight. This acute symptomatic crisis definitively necessitated urgent in-home assessment and cannot safely be delayed until the next regular in-hours general practice review, fully satisfying the criteria for urgent after-hours care.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered subcutaneous morphine and midazolam stat for pain and dyspnea crisis. Adjusted baseline transdermal opioid patch and revised the breakthrough medication protocol.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you feel the pain starting to build (trigger), administer the breakthrough sublingual liquid immediately (behavior), which will prevent the pain from reaching a crisis level (positive consequence). This empowers you to stay ahead of the pain rather than chasing it.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to community palliative care nursing for daily syringe driver management and symptom monitoring.
DME/Safety (Optional): Provision of an oxygen concentrator for comfort and removal of loose rugs.
Follow-up: Community nursing review in 12 hours; routine GP review in 48 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient develops severe terminal agitation, cannot be roused to take sublingual medication, or if breakthrough doses fail to relieve pain within 45 minutes, the caregiver must contact the 24/7 palliative care triage line immediately or call 000 if directed.

